A||||12||FieroPerformance||Turning odometer to 0?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||10-02-2001||06:59 PM||||I am putting a 120 mph speedo into my 85 GT and I want to turn it back to 0 so it will read the mileage on my rebuilt engine. Has anyone done this? If so, how? Do you just push it back from the front with your finger or is there another way?<P>Thanks,<BR>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||shawnster84||10-02-2001||07:15 PM||||Make sure you go to a dealer and get a odmeter sticker that you put on the inside of your door with the real mileage on it..You will have a hard time trying to sell it if you don't and possibly it might be  illegal...nobody wants to buy a car that they don't know the real mileage of..just a thought..||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||10-02-2001||07:21 PM||||Exactly. I don't see any compelling reason to do this, but it can be done. You must completely remove the odo number wheel, remove the lock on back and fiddle with it until it reads correctly.  <P>Don't bother and avoid the hassle and questions. Just write down the mileage at the rebuild.<P>If you do it by pushing the numbers, the tamper lines will come up and you are likely to get in big trouble.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||10-02-2001||08:05 PM||||I strongly recomend you do not do this. Dialing an odometer is illegal and believe it's a federal thing. Allot of states track this each time the car is registered. <P>Your best bet, legally, is to move the original odo to the new speedometer package. This prevents things like Carfax from barfing on it as well. <P>The only time you can legally change the odo numbers is when the odo is replaced. Depending on where you live, your choices here are to set the new odo to pick up where the dead one left off or record the old mileage and if you install a used odo, the new starting point. This way the actual mileage can be quickly calculated.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroPerformance||10-02-2001||08:19 PM||||How much trouble is it to swap odometers?<P>Instructions please. I have both gauge clusters out of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||10-02-2001||10:07 PM||||It's only illegal if you are trying to represent the new reading to be the original mileage. Just put a sticker on the door saying the odometer has been reset and state the mileage at reset time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||10-02-2001||11:34 PM||||Then why not put a sticker on the door stating when the engine was rebuilt...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||10-03-2001||08:33 AM||||I thought the Fiero was suppose to have a anti-tamper odometer?  At least thats what it says in my manual.  That just hype?||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||10-03-2001||09:06 AM||||Illegal only if you are committing fraud. Most states have a way to mark the odo as invalid right on the title. The door sticker is good too. Georgia has an "exempt" mark which is checked on my 87 GT title even though the odo works fine and agrees with the overall condition at 168k. I am not sure what qualifies the exempt mark.<P>I am not sure on the Fiero, but most GM speedos are the same. VERY CAREFULLY loosen the bezel and odometer mounts. There should be a plastic clip holding the tumblers in line. Again, treating it like brain surgery, carefully lift the plastic retainer and turn the numbers back to zero. Re-install the clip and tighten everything back up. Those things are super brittle just for preventing tampering. I did the same procedure on a 160 speedo that I put in my 77 Grand Prix - only I spun the dials to reflect actual mileage (not that it mattered since it was over 100k).||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||10-03-2001||09:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>I thought the Fiero was suppose to have a anti-tamper odometer...  That just hype?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Not hype - it's true.  The plastic strip in the odometer is designed to break if someone attempts to crudely force the little number wheels aginst their will.  The result:  Little white lines appear between the digits, revealing the lttle white lie  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||10-03-2001||11:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Exactly. I don't see any compelling reason to do this, but it can be done. You must completely remove the odo number wheel, remove the lock on back and fiddle with it until it reads correctly.  <P>Don't bother and avoid the hassle and questions. Just write down the mileage at the rebuild.<P>If you do it by pushing the numbers, the tamper lines will come up and you are likely to get in big trouble.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-02-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds like a real pro!!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||SteveJ||10-03-2001||12:03 PM||||For those of you not old enough or associating with devious persons let me tell you. They used to put a power drill on the speedometer cable a turn back the mileage. You would go past the garage and the drill would be locked on for quite a few hours under the car while everyone went about their more legitimate work. The tearing white lines between the numbers were to detect this.<P>I believe cars over ten years old are exempt from mileage reporting requirements.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||10-03-2001||04:53 PM||||In Pennsylvania, any car more than 10 years old is exempt from odometer disclosure. The certificate of title indicates this. When the car is registered you have the <B>option</B> of stating the mileage for the records. I guess they figure after what a car has been through in 10 years it doesn't matter anymore. That doesn't make it a good idea, but it's not illeagal unless you're trying to defraud someone.<P>I rolled one back to zero once just to see how easy it would be. It is easy. I never put it in a car, I think it's still sitting around my shop somewhere. I'd think it could make it difficult to sell the car, especially with things like carfax around.||||reg||1
